= Counterexample — Technical =

== Predicate logic ==
For universal claim:

<pre>
∀x (P(x) → Q(x))
</pre>

a counterexample is a witness <code>a</code> satisfying:

<pre>
P(a) ∧ ¬Q(a)
</pre>

Existence of one witness proves the universal formula false in the domain.

== Countermodels ==
For argument:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

a countermodel is model <code>M</code> such that:

<pre>
M ⊨ Γ
M ⊭ φ
</pre>

A countermodel establishes failure of semantic entailment.

== Minimal counterexample proof ==
Assume the set of counterexamples is nonempty and well-ordered.

Choose minimal counterexample <code>m</code>.

Use the structure of m to construct smaller counterexample <code>m'</code>:

<pre>
m' < m
</pre>

contradicting minimality.

This technique underlies infinite descent and many induction-style arguments.

== Property-based testing ==
In software testing, property-based systems generate inputs searching for a violation of a universal property.

A failing input is a computational counterexample.

Shrinking algorithms then search for a simpler failing input that exposes the essential defect.

Testing cannot establish a universal property over unbounded inputs, but found counterexamples decisively refute the tested claim.

== Model checking ==
For temporal/property formula φ:

<pre>
M ⊭ φ
</pre>

a model checker can often produce a path/trace demonstrating violation.

That trace is a counterexample relative to the model.

If the model is an abstraction, the trace can be spurious and must be checked against the concrete system.

== Counterexample-guided abstraction refinement ==
CEGAR loop:

<pre>
abstract
→ verify
→ counterexample
→ check spurious?
→ refine abstraction
→ repeat
</pre>

The process terminates when the property is proved at the abstraction level or a genuine counterexample is found, subject to tool/model limits.

== Statistical non-counterexamples ==
For probabilistic hypothesis H:

<pre>
P(E | H) > 0
</pre>

observing E is logically compatible with H.

Evidence against H must be assessed using likelihood, probability, model comparison, or another inferential framework rather than treating one low-probability event as a formal counterexample.

